    "case_summary": "A patient in their 60s presented to the emergency department with paroxysmal chest pain. The chest pain had occurred 5 times during the day, and each episode lasted 15 to 30 minutes.\n\nOn arrival, the patient's blood pressure and pulse rate were 106/52 mm Hg and 64 beats per minutes, respectively. Chest auscultation revealed normal breath and heart sounds with no murmurs.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  • Hemogram, serum electrolyte levels, kidney and liver function tests, B-type natriuretic peptide and D-dimer levels were all within normal limits\n  • Cardiac troponin I level was 25 ng/mL (normal, <0.023 ng/mL)\n\n- ECG Findings:\n  Image A Title: Initial ECG\n  Image A Description: Revealed sinus rhythm, ST-segment elevation in leads III and aVR, Q wave in lead III, and ST-segment depression in leads I, II, aVL, aVF, and V2 to V6\n\n  Image B Title: Repeated ECG (taken during recurrent chest pain on second day)\n  Image B Description: Reveals sinus bradycardia (46 beats per minute), Q-wave amplitude deepening, ST-segment falling back to equipotential line, and T-wave inversion in lead III\n\n- Coronary Angiography:\n  Findings showed total occlusion of the right coronary artery (RCA) and subtotal occlusion of the left main coronary artery (LMCA)",
